course: biology
lesson: 389
title: How scientists test animal minds: the mirror test, puzzle boxes and Clever Hans
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Put a sticker on your forehead, look in the mirror and you pull it off at once: you know the face in the mirror is you. In the mirror test scientists put a mark on an animal where it can see it only in a mirror and watch whether it touches the mark on its own body.
explain: Over a hundred years ago a horse called Clever Hans seemed to do sums by tapping his hoof. A scientist showed that Hans was watching tiny, unknowing movements of the people around him: they relaxed when he reached the right number, and he stopped.
explain: Since then scientists make sure the person testing an animal does not know the answer, so nobody can give hints by accident.
explain: A fair test must also suit the animal. A dog lives by its nose, so a mirror means little to it, but it can tell its own smell from another dog's.
explain: Good scientists repeat tests with many animals in other labs, use rewards, let animals choose to take part and never hurt them.
fact: In the mirror test a mark is put where an animal can see it only in a mirror; touching the mark on its own body suggests it knows the reflection is itself.
fact: Great apes, dolphins, one elephant and some magpies are reported to pass the mirror test, while most monkeys, dogs and cats do not.
fact: Dogs know the world mostly by smell, and one scientist reported that dogs spend less time sniffing their own wee than another dog's.
fact: Over a hundred years ago a scientist put cats in puzzle boxes and timed them; they got faster with each try, learning by trial and error.
fact: Clever Hans tapped right answers by reading tiny movements of the people watching, so he was clever at reading people, not at maths.
fact: Today the person testing an animal often does not know the answer, so they cannot give hints by accident.
fact: In the water jar test, based on an old fable, rooks and crows dropped stones into a tube until the water rose with a floating treat; children usually pass it at about seven or eight.
fact: In a test reported in 2021, cuttlefish waited for their favourite shrimp instead of eating a less tasty crab at once, which needs self-control.
fact: Alex the grey parrot learned to name colours and shapes, say the number of objects up to about six and say «none» when two things had no difference.
fact: When Alex named a thing correctly he was often given that very thing as his reward; scientists argue how much he understood, but he showed parrots can do far more than copy sounds.
fact: One clever animal might be special, or a test might have a mistake, so tests are repeated with many animals and in other labs.
fact: Good animal tests use rewards, let animals choose to take part and never hurt them, and many countries have rules to protect animals in science.
exercise: What does the mirror test check? | Whether an animal knows the reflection is itself | Whether an animal likes the way it looks in the glass | Whether an animal can see all the colours of the rainbow | Whether mirrors scare wild animals away from the food
exercise: Where is the mark put in the mirror test? | Where the animal can see it only in a mirror | On its front paw, where it can easily see it without help | On the mirror itself | Inside its mouth
exercise: Which animals are reported to pass the mirror test? | Great apes, dolphins, one elephant and some magpies | All dogs and cats | Only humans | Every monkey, every parrot and every pig ever tested in a zoo
exercise: Why might a clever dog fail the mirror test? | It knows the world mostly by smell, not sight | Dogs are blind to their own reflection in glass | Dogs hate stickers | Dogs never look forward
exercise: How did one scientist test dogs fairly? | By how long they sniffed their own wee compared with another's | By giving them a mirror twice | By asking them to bark their names | By timing them on a running track against cats, horses and other dogs
exercise: What did cats in old puzzle boxes show? | They got faster with each try, learning by trial and error | They opened the box on the first try every time, without any practice | They never got out | They read the instructions
exercise: How did Clever Hans really get the answers? | He watched tiny movements of the people around him | He could really add and subtract numbers like a pupil | Someone hid nearby and whispered the numbers to him | He counted with a hidden abacus under the hay in his stable
exercise: Why do testers now often not know the answer? | So they cannot give hints by accident | To make the test harder for them to mark | Because they are new and still learning the job | So they can guess the answers too, along with the animals
exercise: What did rooks and crows do in the water jar test? | Dropped stones in until the water and the treat rose | Drank all the water | Broke the jar with their beaks | Waited for the treat to sink, then pulled the jar over with their feet
exercise: At about what age do children usually pass the water jar test? | About seven or eight | About two, before they can talk | About fifteen, as teenagers | Never, not even as grown-ups
exercise: What did cuttlefish do in the waiting test? | Waited for their favourite shrimp instead of eating crab at once | Ate everything at once | Refused all food | Ate the scientist's sandwich when he left it on the side of the tank
exercise: What does waiting for a better treat need? | Self-control | Very sharp teeth | A big strong body | Very good hearing
exercise: What could Alex the parrot do? | Name colours and shapes and count objects up to about six | Read whole books | Do long division | Only copy sounds he heard, without ever knowing what any of them meant
exercise: Why shouldn't we trust a test done on only one animal? | That one might be special or the test might have a mistake | One animal always lies | One animal is too few to feed | Tests on one animal are against the law in every country of the world
exercise: What does a good animal test do? | Uses rewards and never hurts the animal | Frightens the animal to make it try harder each time | Keeps the animal hungry for several days first | Forces the animal to take part, even if it is scared
test: «my dog barks at the mirror then ignores it, so dogs are dumb» What is the best reasoning? | A dog lives by smell, so a mirror test does not suit it | Yes, dogs are dumb | Dogs cannot see at all | Dogs know mirrors are fake so they ignore all tests
test: A horse taps the right answer only when its owner, who knows the sum, stands in front of it. What is the best next step? | Test it again with a person who does not know the answer | Give the horse a medal at once and put it on television | Ask the owner to stand closer | Make the sums harder
test: In a cartoon a cat solves a maths test faster than the class. If a real scientist saw a cat do this, what should they do first? | Check for hidden hints and repeat the test fairly | Believe it straight away and tell the newspapers | Make the cat a teacher | Ban maths for cats
test: Count them: a chart lists 5 kinds that passed the mirror test and 3 that failed. How many kinds were tested? | Eight | Five | Three | Two
test: Count them: the water jar test is done with 4 crows and then 3 rooks. How many birds in all, and how many legs? | 7 birds, 14 legs | 7 birds, 28 legs | 12 birds, 14 legs | 7 birds, 7 legs
test: «a crow got a treat outta a tube with stones, it's a fluke» What would make the result stronger? | Many crows doing it again, in other labs too | One crow doing it once more | Telling a friend about it | Painting the tube red so the crows can see it better
test: «why'd they reward Alex with the thing he named, lol spoilt bird» What is the best reason? | So the word he said connected with the real thing | To make him sleep | Because parrots eat everything they can name | To make him stop talking so loudly in the lab
test: What would happen if a scientist always smiled when the animal got near the right answer? | The animal might learn to read the smile, not solve the task | The animal would solve it better for real, because it would feel happy and calm | Nothing, animals never notice faces | The scientist would get the answer wrong
test: «put a sticker on a cat's paw, it pulls it off, so cats pass the mirror test» What is wrong? | It could see the paw without a mirror, so the test means nothing | Nothing is wrong, cats pass | The paw is too small for a sticker | Cats have no paws
test: «are these animals in tests treated ok or what» What is the right picture? | Good tests use rewards, let animals choose and never hurt them | All tests hurt animals | Animals are never tested | Tests are only done on toy animals, never on real living ones
test: Why did Clever Hans still deserve the name clever? | He was very good at reading people's tiny movements | He could do real sums in his head | He could talk | He could write numbers in the sand with his hoof
exam: Identify the principal flaw disclosed in the celebrated case of the horse Clever Hans. | The animal responded to involuntary cues from the persons present | The horse was in fact a different animal | The questions were posed in a foreign language which the horse did not know | The horse was unable to hear the questions
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the mirror test may be deemed unsuited to the assessment of dogs. | The species perceives the world principally by the sense of smell | Dogs are incapable of sight | Dogs are prohibited from laboratories by law in most countries of Europe | Dogs invariably pass all tests
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the water jar test administered to rooks. | The birds dropped stones into the tube until the floating reward rose within reach | The birds drank the water to reach the reward | The birds broke the vessel with their beaks | The birds failed to obtain the reward on every occasion
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises sound practice in experiments upon animal cognition? | Results are repeated with many animals and in other laboratories, without harm | A single remarkable individual suffices to establish a conclusion beyond any doubt | The tester should invariably know and display the answer | Animals should be compelled to participate
